      ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{(-2)x+(1)y}{3}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-2,-3)$,$(-1,2)$,$(-2,1)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,-3)}=\\frac{(-2)(-2)+(1)(-3)}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,2)}=\\frac{(-2)(-1)+(1)(2)}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,1)}=\\frac{(-2)(-2)+(1)(1)}{3}=\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172038/workspace/evidence/asset-reference-repair/separate/deep/outputs-b/db9c58c03ae2d47accc81a663a59d975daf5cd55fc4bb40e440ec01af9d2c54b.png)",
